#
# All platforms: do not build volume shaders a driver has no room for.
#
# QtDataVisualization compiles four volume-rendering programs for every graph on
# the desktop path, in Abstract3DRenderer::initShaders(), whether or not that
# graph will ever hold a QCustom3DVolume. Three of them declare
#
#   uniform highp vec4 colorIndex[256];      (texture3d.frag:7 and two others)
#
# which is 1024 fragment uniform components in that one array. A driver only has
# to offer GL_MAX_FRAGMENT_UNIFORM_COMPONENTS of them, and Mesa 10.0 on VMware's
# SVGA3D - the driver Windows XP gets under VMware - offers 896:
#
#   GL_VERSION                          2.1 Mesa 10.0 (git-5da4fa2)
#   GL_MAX_FRAGMENT_UNIFORM_COMPONENTS  896
#
# 1024 > 896, so the link fails whatever else the program contains:
#
#   error: Too many fragment shader default uniform block components
#
# ShaderHelper::initialize() ignores the result of link() and goes on to look up
# some thirty uniform and attribute locations, each of which prints "shader
# program is not linked". Four programs per graph, three graphs in the gallery:
# the window comes up behind a screenful of scrolling warnings, which is what
# this looked like from the outside - a graph that would not start rather than a
# feature that would not fit.
#
# This only became reachable once all/04 stopped calling every Mesa driver an ES2
# driver. That patch is right - the driver is desktop GL 2.1 with GLSL 1.20 - and
# this is the real limit hiding behind the vendor-string test it removed. The
# fix is the same in kind: ask the driver, do not guess. If the advertised
# fragment uniform limit is already too small for the shader, do not even try to
# link it; otherwise link the program and believe the answer.
#
# Volume rendering is then simply absent on such a driver, which is honest - it
# could not have worked - and everything else on the desktop path stays. Nothing
# in this kit's tests uses QCustom3DVolume, and a graph that has no volume item
# never touched these programs in the first place.
#
# Changes:
#   qtdatavis3d/src/datavisualization/utils/shaderhelper.cpp
#       initialize(): check link(), report the log once, and stop before the
#       location lookups instead of printing one warning per lookup
#   qtdatavis3d/src/datavisualization/utils/shaderhelper_p.h
#       isInitialized(): let the caller see that a program did not link
#   qtdatavis3d/src/datavisualization/engine/abstract3drenderer.cpp
#       initVolumeTextureShaders(): drop all four programs if the first will not
#       link, and say so once
#       renderCustomItems(): draw a volume item through the volume path only when
#       that path exists, via a single drawAsVolume flag replacing the three
#       separate isVolume() tests that decided it
#
#       initVolumeTextureShaders(): once the answer is known, skip the attempt
#       entirely - a renderer is re-initialised on every shadow-quality change,
#       and where the graph's surface is rebuilt on each tab switch that is what
#       turned one unsupported feature into a scrolling wall of warnings
#
#       The answer is now remembered process-wide, not just per renderer
#       instance. Windows XP / VMware can destroy and recreate graph windows
#       while switching tabs, which creates new renderers; letting each one
#       relink the same oversized volume shaders disturbed the neighbouring
#       Bars and Scatter renderers even though no QCustom3DVolume was present.
#   qtdatavis3d/src/datavisualization/engine/abstract3drenderer_p.h
#       m_volumeShadersUnsupported, and clearVolumeTextureShaders() for the
#       teardown both paths now need
#
# Apply: git apply, from the Qt source root (qt-everywhere-src-5.15.19/)
